cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If this instrument recovered something you were about to lose, cite the version you ran -- not the idea."
title: "git-complement-audit: auditing every path Git keeps outside HEAD"
abstract: >-
  Zero-dependency CLI that measures what a Git repository keeps outside HEAD -- unmerged
  commits behind hidden refs, detached worktrees nobody adopted, paths a reset dropped, blobs
  no ref can name -- classifies every path in that complement, and says whether anything in
  it is worth saving before 'git worktree prune' or 'git gc --prune' destroys it. The audit
  reads: it writes no file and mutates no ref, and the only writes are the opt-in --receipt
  and --rescue-dir a caller asks for. Exit codes are a contract (0 clean, 1 orphaned code or
  secret risk, 2 invalid repository or arguments), and every pass that is skipped is declared
  in the report, so a clean verdict never covers bytes the instrument did not look at.
authors:
